      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1863 Union troops abandoned Forsyth, burning the town to keep it from Confederate capture.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://forsythmo.gov/history",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Contractor Selsor rebuilt the courthouse in 1855, adding a 40‑by‑50‑foot stone structure with a 10‑by‑12‑foot tower.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.forsythmissouri.org/historyofforsythmissouri",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The town’s public library, a cornerstone of community learning, remains a key landmark in Forsyth.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forsyth%2C_Missouri",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Forsyth originally sat on the White River’s banks, now near Shadowrock Park, reflecting its river‑town heritage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
